## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming data often requires maintaining a running statistic under insertions and deletions. The median is robust and a common interview target for dynamic data structures.

## Key Concepts
- Two-heap approach: keep lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap.
- Duplicates supported; lazy deletion via a delay map.
- Rebalancing invariant: size difference at most 1; median derived from heap tops.

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianDS {
  constructor(){
    this.lo = new MaxHeap(); // lower half
    this.hi = new MinHeap(); // upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();
    this.sizeLo = 0;
    this.sizeHi = 0;
  }
  _cleanTop(h) {
    const top = h.peek();
    while (top !== null && this.delayed.get(top) > 0) {
      h.pop();
      this.delayed.set(top, this.delayed.get(top) - 1);
    }
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0 || x <= this.lo.peek()) {
      this.lo.push(x); this.sizeLo++;
    } else { this.hi.push(x); this.sizeHi++; }
    this._balance();
  }
  remove(x){
    const loTop = this.lo.peek();
    if (loTop !== null && x <= loTop) {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeLo--;
      if (x === loTop) this._cleanTop(this.lo);
    } else {
      this.delayed.set(x, (this.delayed.get(x) || 0) + 1);
      this.sizeHi--;
      this._cleanTop(this.hi);
    }
    this._balance();
  }
  _balance(){
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi + 1) {
      this.hi.push(this.lo.pop()); this.sizeLo--; this.sizeHi++;
    } else if (this.sizeHi > this.sizeLo) {
      this.lo.push(this.hi.pop()); this.sizeHi--; this.sizeLo++;
    }
    this._cleanTop(this.lo); this._cleanTop(this.hi);
  }
  getMedian(){
    if (this.sizeLo === 0) return null;
    if (this.sizeLo > this.sizeHi) return this.lo.peek();
    return (this.lo.peek() + this.hi.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic connectivity with insertions and deletions is a classic hard problem. This offline approach tests understanding of edge lifetimes and versioning, and how to combine segment trees with DSU rollback for log-scale updates.

## Key Concepts
- DSU with rollback (no path compression) to enable undo.
- Segment tree over time to map edge active intervals to time ranges.
- Offline processing: traverse tree, apply unions, answer leaf queries, then rollback.

## Code Example
```javascript
class RollbackDSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.sz = Array(n).fill(1);
    this.ch = [];
  }
  find(x){ while (this.parent[x]!==x) x = this.parent[x]; return x; }
  union(a,b){
    a = this.find(a); b = this.find(b);
    if (a===b){ this.ch.push(null); return; }
    if (this.sz[a] < this.sz[b]) [a,b] = [b,a];
    this.ch.push([b, this.parent[b], a, this.sz[a]]);
    this.parent[b] = a; this.sz[a] += this.sz[b];
  }
  snapshot(){ return this.ch.length; }
  rollback(snap){
    while (this.ch.length > snap){
      const ch = this.ch.pop();
      if (!ch) continue;
      const [b, oldP, a, oldS] = ch;
      this.parent[b] = oldP; this.sz[a] = oldS;
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of augmented binary search trees to support order statistics—a fundamental data structures concept.

## Key Concepts

- **Augmented BST/Treap**: Each node stores value, count (for duplicates), and subtreeSize
- **Order statistics**: getKthSmallest navigates using left subtree size and node counts
- **Duplicate handling**: Multiple identical values stored in a single node with a count field
- **Rebalancing**: Operations maintain O(log n) time via rotations or split/merge

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(v) {
    this.v = v;
    this.c = 1;      // count of this value
    this.sz = 1;     // subtree size
    this.l = null;
    this.r = null;
    this.p = null;  // priority for treap
  }

  upd() {
    this.sz = (this.l?.sz || 0) + (this.r?.sz || 0) + this.c;
  }
}

function kth(node, k) {
  if (!node) return null;
  const leftSize = node.l?.sz || 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) return kth(node.l, k);
  if (k <= leftSize + node.c) return node.v;
  return kth(node.r, k - leftSize - node.c);
}
```

## Time Complexity

